WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
All right, I'm gonna walk you through a demo of SmallDocs.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.080
Gonna start off with a little mini demo

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.360
and explain why SmallDocs exists and how it works.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.460
Then I'm gonna show you some demos of regular usage,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.160
which show you how to create documents with your agents

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.440
and also how to read code with your agents.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.800
And then I'm gonna explain why SmallDocs is private.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.800
And then I'm gonna show you some advanced demos

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.440
for people who are enjoying the first part and wanna see the real full set

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.200
of SmallDocs capabilities.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.820
So SmallDocs is a way for you to work with your agents

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.120
to produce easy to read, easy to share documents.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.760
So here I just asked Claude to s.me@poem and it's opened the document in the browser

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.520
and I can generate a little short link to share it.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.280
And even though it's in the browser, it's private and I'm gonna explain that later.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.040
So agents have really changed the way that we work.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.200
You can really tackle all different types of work

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.440
with an agent and SmallDocs needs to handle

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.800
that flexibility too and it's built to do that.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.060
So with SmallDocs, you can build presentations,

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:13.120
which can be very, very complicated. You can do financial analysis

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.920
and look at data in a really powerful way.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.900
For example, you can use the traditional calculations.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.300
You can build interactive charts

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.520
and you can create these mermaid diagrams,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.640
but you don't create them, your agent does,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.740
but it's about your agent being able to express itself

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:38.740
to handle the broad topic of topics of work that we now do with our agents

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.020
because they are a great way to get analytical and creation work done.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.320
So how does this work?

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.560
SmallDocs is actually a CLI. You just s.doc and then a path to a file

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.680
and it's that that opens it in the browser.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.780
But typically you don't actually do this, normally your agents do it.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.860
And they're able to do it because when we install SmallDocs,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.040
when you install it via a CLI, we go through and we go into your root agent folder.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.740
So when you install Cloud Code, for example, you get a Cloud folder.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.960
When you install Codex, you get a Codex folder.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.400
Inside of that folder, there's a Cloud.md file

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.440
in the Cloud folder and in the Codex folder, there's an agents.md file.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.600
And these files, if you put information in them, it's global context,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.440
which all of your agent sessions will use.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.080
And so here, if we look inside of the Cloud.md file,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.480
we can see we've added something about SmallDocs

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.240
and the same is true for the agents.md file.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.920
And this means that all of those agent sessions understand that SmallDocs exist

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.640
and they understand a bit about how to use it.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.960
And they also understand what commands they need to run

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.560
if they're gonna use it to produce something powerful. So if they wanna build slides,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.540
they'll first run s.slides and that gives them the documentation

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:59.680
that they need to build slides. So that documentation is not always in your,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.760
in those agent folders. That's just, they just know they need to run s.slides

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.880
if they wanna get information on how to build slides

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.880
that render in SmallDocs, for example.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.040
Okay.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.400
So let's look at some demos now of the kind of stuff

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.040
that you can do and I do with SmallDocs.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.960
So I'm an engineer and SmallDocs is a great way for you to get your agent

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.200
to teach you conceptual engineering things. So for example, here,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.220
I've downloaded the browser use repository and I wanna understand its architecture.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:34.000
And maybe for example, I wanna, I want to,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.280
I don't necessarily wanna do this in my computer

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.000
and I wanna generate a short link to myself and go and read it on a park bench.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.000
But it allows the agent to explain things

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.560
and for you to ingest things in a kind of more natural

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.400
and calm and easy way than in a kind of narrow terminal window

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.020
that isn't always great to work in.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.560
And, for example, you can use code to do financial analysis

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.480
or data analysis and SmallDocs is really good for that.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.720
So here, I'm just looking at some dividend returns and it can pick up data

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.360
and produce complex data analysis

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.360
with sheets that I can then kind of work through my own time

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.640
basically or share with someone on my team.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.720
So let's look at using it in codecs.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.400
So here, I've asked codecs to produce a presentation

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.000
on the don't repeat yourself coding concept

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.440
in Patagonia's branding just to show you some of the stuff that SmallDocs can do.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.740
And let's just wait for codecs to respond.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.960
And so here, it's opened up and created this document.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:48.200
And the cool thing is you can start off in inside of a Small Doc,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.120
but then you can actually export to the traditional formats you're used to.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.680
So it's a cool way to begin working on a slide deck.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.880
And so another thing that's changed with agents

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.360
is that we, in a way we've left our IDEs behind.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.360
So if you're used to working with an agent in a terminal,

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.840
just open your coding IDE much less than you did. And SmallDocs is there to step in

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.500
if you need to read code files.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.680
So you can always just say to your agent s.me a code file

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.640
and it will open nice in your browser

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.080
and we have a little language specific summary functionality

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.240
which collapses files, but still allows you to ingest

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.800
kind of what's the headline structure of this file.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.480
And we can also do things like get your agent can also annotate code

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.480
and it can create a pathway through some code.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.360
So for example, I wanna create a code walkthrough of how we rate limit a request.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.180
And here it's opened two files, the API and the rate limiter.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.560
And it's given me some steps that I can follow starting off in one file,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.560
now jumping to the rate limiter file and jumping back to the API file.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.840
So it's a way for me to, for you and your agent

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.440
to kind of teach each other stuff and understand code.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.600
So the files that I'm talking about

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.040
are actually like the most private files you have.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.200
Markdown files that you're working on,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.760
they might contain live information about a bug

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.340
and obviously your coding files are highly sensitive.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.380
So privacy is paramount with SmallDocs.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.480
And we achieve that by using something called

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.880
the URL fragment, which is this little hash that you can see here.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.000
And so you'll see that there's this hash and then there's a big long string of text.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.880
This long string of text is actually your underlying document

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.840
effectively basically encoded, which is what the CLI does

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.120
and put into the URL beyond this little hash which is the URL fragment.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:51.120
And the UI fragment, it is not sent to the server basically.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.000
So this is MDN docs, it's processed by the client,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.280
the browser after the resource is received. So when you make a request, the SmallDocs

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.160
we're actually doing is just fetching a JavaScript rendering engine,

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:10.440
which knows how to kind of decompress these strings and render them as files,

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.620
as things you can actually interpret basically.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.920
So we're not, this data isn't being sent to SmallDocs.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.440
SmallDocs is just rendering whatever is in this hidden URL fragment.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.440
We take privacy very seriously and we have a little verified check,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.480
which checks that the code that you're actually receiving

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:30.280
is the code that's in our open source repository.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.240
So you know that we're not doing anything spooky behind the scenes.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.240
And if you create a short link,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.200
to create a short link that doesn't have your full document

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.280
in it, we do need to send something to our server.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.600
But to ensure this is extremely secure, we, on your clients, on the browser,

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.180
we encrypt your document and we get a decryption key,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.840
which is this little key K here, basically. This is the decryption key.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.360
The data that the ciphertext, so the encrypted text is sent to our server,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.000
which is saved under this ID. But it's only legible if it's combined

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.100
with this decryption key, which stays behind the URL fragment.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.100
So we're using that same concept twice,

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.480
once with the full document, which is the standard usage,

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.200
and then once with the decryption key,

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.660
which is how we allow you to create short links.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.960
So time to look at some more advanced features of SmallDocs now.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.760
So the file that we've been working with,

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.200
even though they're pointing at a local file in our computer,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.760
we're not actually doing a live sync between the two.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.520
So if I was to edit this file here in SmallDocs,

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.320
it would not actually edit the underlying file

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.520
on the computer, but you can create a bridge between your local machine and the file.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.000
So the key word that your agent is looking for, in that case, is the word bridge.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.540
So here I can say sdocme, a launch brief with a bridge, and it's gonna open up.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.920
And you can see here that it says local sync is enabled, basically.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:08.040
And what that means is, so for example here, this is me catting this file.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.640
So you can see that the H1 is Q3 launch brief, which you can see here.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:18.520
But if I edit this and change it to Q4 launch brief, and I now cat this again,

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.880
you can see that that change has flowed back to the document.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.720
So we're working on things together. So let me go back to read mode.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.800
And this also means we have a little comment functionality,

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.360
which is a nice way to give feedback on things.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.680
So you can either leave comments like this or inline comments.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.040
And so, for example, you can do things like change to read.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:52.460
And I can tell the agent I've left some comments.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.880
And it can then pick up those comments in the file

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.840
in a live way because we're sharing data

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.280
and those comments add information to the file.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.280
So here it's reading that and it will make a change.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.600
So you can see it's making a change.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.200
And you can see that's come through now.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.560
So we're kind of working together on a document.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.720
You can, if you wanna just leave comments,

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:23.640
another way you can give comments to the agent is,

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.640
say, for example, if I wanted to delete this paragraph,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.920
you can then just copy with comments. For example, if I haven't got a bridge open

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.040
and paste that into the document and it comes with your comment there

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.000
and the agent will then act on that as well.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:48.540
So that's comments in a bridge. And now feedback.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.960
Another thing you can do is your agent

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.240
can create an interactive HTML form in a small doc

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.200
and then run a process in the background that's waiting for you to give it feedback.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.440
So it's a way of, if you wanna scope out something quite complicated with your agent,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.640
it can do it in a way that's more flexible than doing so in the browser.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.360
For here, for example, we wanna s.feedback

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.620
the table of content sidebar feature plan with me. So coming up with some feature.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.640
It's then gonna open that. And you can see here that it's given us

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.200
an interactive HTML form to give feedback on

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.840
and it's running a process in the background. So it's waiting for us to submit this

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.720
and that's gonna kill the process and give the data back to the agent.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:41.080
So I think I'm gonna do this. I'll move it to the left and build it in red.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.480
And then I've sent that back. The session has ended and you can see now

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:52.140
it's picked up the scope that we have given it basically.

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:57.800
So yeah, you can see it built in red. So it's got that feedback from us.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:05.000
And the final thing is that we create so much markdown these days that,

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.960
and SmallDocs is opening all these markdown files for you

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.320
'cause it's a nice way to read them, but it also comes with a markdown library.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.920
And this allows you to, for example,

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.920
filter things by project, search for things like brief.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:26.160
And you can tag things if you want and filter by date and star files.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:27.880
And we also have a little thing called a rescued,

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:31.440
which is basically when your Mac creates a markdown file

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.600
in a folder or when your agent creates a markdown file

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.920
in like a temp folder that's gonna delete itself,

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.720
we add it to like a local kind of directory in SmallDocs on your computer,

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.640
which means that if it's been s-docked to be opened, which means that it's not lost.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:49.000
So you won't go back and be like, ah, that was written in my temp folder, that plan,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.080
and now it's gone, we've saved it for you.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:58.720
So that is SmallDocs in its current form. It's open source on GitHub.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:04.880
Please check out the repo. And if you wanna build features, make a pull request,

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:10.220
and we also have a Discord that you can join and we'd love to talk with you.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:15.020
For businesses, it's totally free to use for individuals.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:20.080
You can install it and get going with it. For businesses, get in touch

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:26.900
and we can do interesting things like cloud shared libraries

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.800
and doing things on custom domains and custom branding and stuff like that.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:36.440
So yeah, enjoy using SmallDocs and thanks for listening.
